In the course Laboratory Assignments in Operations Research II at Aalto (autumn 2026), a partner and I controlled a
steam power plant. The course gave the plant as a Simulink model. A boiler feeds a high-pressure header. The turbine
passes steam to a back-pressure header, and the consumers take their steam from there. A steam accumulator stores
steam and gives it back.

I tuned the PID controller of the fuel feed and a PI controller on the turbine valve, which holds the back pressure.
Then I tuned the accumulator's controllers and tested the whole plant against changes in the consumption. My partner
designed state-feedback controllers for the same plant.

The models open below. Double-click a subsystem to go inside, rest the pointer on a block to read its parameters,
and click a block to see what each parameter evaluates to. Ctrl and the scroll wheel zoom; on a phone, use two fingers. I drew
them with slxkit, a tool I wrote to view, edit and simulate Simulink models without MATLAB.

